<!-- Synthetic teaching example. Review and load on a disposable Wazuh 4.x manager.
     Check IDs are unused.

     Written after PHP's 21 October 2026 emergency release for CVE-2026-109618 and
     CVE-2026-109619 (nicknamed "PHP2Shell"), two unauthenticated RCE bugs in PHP
     core affecting PHP-FPM, mod_php, php-cgi, FrankenPHP and LiteSpeed. Fixed in
     8.5.12, 8.4.27, 8.3.36 and 8.2.35. This file contains no exploit code: it is
     post-compromise indicator patterns to adapt to your own decoders and log
     sources, then prove out with `ruleguard run` before trusting them.

     Verification history:
       - 0.3.2: not checked against a live engine.
       - 0.3.3: proven to parse and load on a live Wazuh 4.14.7 manager
         (`wazuh-analysisd -t` exits 0). That only means the file loads, not that
         any rule fires correctly.
       - 0.3.4: the rules below were adapted against findings from testing on a
         live Wazuh 4.14.7 manager with `wazuh-logtest` and synthetic sample
         events (no PoC, no real exploit chain, no real FIM event, no production
         log data): 6 cases that should alert fired at the expected level, 11
         normal-looking events did not alert, and 1 regression check on an older
         rule still hit. That testing was not run by whoever last edited this
         comment; it is reported here because it changed what the rules say.
         Re-run it yourself with `wazuh-logtest` against your own decoders
         before you trust any of this on a real host.

     Confidence of each base rule:
       - 554 ("File added to the system") and 1010 / 5108 (kernel segfault / OOM
         lines) are documented Wazuh default rules.
       - There is no single documented default rule for "a shell was spawned by
         this parent process" across every Wazuh install. auditd's execve
         decoder does not produce a "parent_process" field at all, only a
         numeric ppid, so a rule written against "parent_process" can never
         fire; rule 100211 below uses audit.exe and audit.cwd instead, which
         auditd really decodes, as a parent proxy (a specific tool launched
         from inside the web docroot), not a verified parent-process check.
       - Stock rule 80792 ("Audit: Command") only fires when audit.key is
         listed in etc/lists/audit-keys with the value "command"; a rule
         written as a child of 80792 for a custom key can silently never fire.
         Rule 100211 below chains from <if_group>audit</if_group> instead, to
         avoid that trap. If you would rather chain from 80792, add your audit
         key to that list first and prove it fires with wazuh-logtest. -->
<group name="php2shell_indicators,">
  <!-- 1. A new PHP (or other executable) file appears inside a web root. A legitimate
       deploy writes these as the deploy user, not as www-data/php-fpm/apache at request
       time, so this is a plausible webshell-drop signal. if_sid 554 is Wazuh's
       documented default FIM rule for a newly added file (enable alert_new_files in
       your syscheck config). Level kept low (5): a site that deploys often adds PHP
       files as routine, legitimate traffic, so this is a lead to review, not an
       incident on its own. On a per-user PHP-FPM setup, files are owned by the site's
       own account, not www-data/apache/nginx: drop the uname_after filter there, or
       this rule will simply never match. -->
  <rule id="100210" level="5">
    <if_sid>554</if_sid>
    <field name="file" type="pcre2">\.(php|phtml|php5)$</field>
    <field name="uname_after" type="pcre2">^(www-data|php-fpm|apache|nginx)$</field>
    <description>Demo: new PHP file written to disk by the web server's own account. Drop the
      uname_after field on a per-user PHP-FPM setup where this account is never the owner.</description>
    <mitre><id>T1505.003</id></mitre>
  </rule>

  <!-- 2. A specific outbound tool launched from inside the web docroot, via Linux audit
       (auditd). "Any shell from the web root" is very noisy: PHP's own exec() and most
       frameworks already run commands through /bin/sh as routine behavior, not just
       after a compromise. This matches narrower, higher-signal tools instead (nc,
       ncat), which a plain PHP app has little legitimate reason to launch.
       audit.cwd is a placeholder for YOUR web docroot path; replace it before loading.
       Needs the full audit record group (not a single line) for audit.cwd to decode;
       a partial sample will not show this field in wazuh-logtest. -->
  <rule id="100211" level="13">
    <if_group>audit</if_group>
    <field name="audit.exe" type="pcre2">/(nc|ncat)$</field>
    <field name="audit.cwd" type="pcre2">^/var/www/REPLACE_WITH_YOUR_DOCROOT</field>
    <description>Demo: nc or ncat launched from inside a web docroot. Replace audit.cwd with your
      own docroot path; this has not been checked against any decoder but auditd's.</description>
    <mitre><id>T1059.004</id></mitre>
  </rule>

  <!-- 3. A burst of php-fpm worker crashes or out-of-memory kills in a short window.
       Both CVEs are memory-corruption bugs (OOM cleanup and use-after-free), so
       repeated crashes right before a webshell appears is a plausible precursor, not
       proof on its own, pair it with rules 100210/100211 above.
       php-fpm's own log line is often not collected at all; what usually reaches the
       manager is the KERNEL's line about the crash, which stock Wazuh already decodes
       as rule 1010 (segfault) or 5108 (OOM kill). 100212 and 100214 are single-event
       rules chained from those stock rules and narrowed to php-fpm; 100213 is the
       frequency rule that counts repeats of EITHER one via their shared "php2shell_crash"
       group (never its own group: this outer file group is "php2shell_indicators", a
       different name, so it cannot count itself). -->
  <rule id="100212" level="5">
    <if_sid>1010</if_sid>
    <field name="message" type="pcre2">php-fpm</field>
    <group>php2shell_crash,</group>
    <description>Demo: a kernel segfault line naming php-fpm.</description>
  </rule>
  <rule id="100214" level="5">
    <if_sid>5108</if_sid>
    <field name="message" type="pcre2">php-fpm</field>
    <group>php2shell_crash,</group>
    <description>Demo: a kernel OOM-kill line naming php-fpm.</description>
  </rule>
  <rule id="100213" level="8" frequency="5" timeframe="120">
    <if_matched_group>php2shell_crash</if_matched_group>
    <description>Demo: 5 or more php-fpm crashes or OOM kills (rules 100212/100214) within 2 minutes.</description>
  </rule>
</group>
